// Practice quiz: Environment variables and keeping secrets out of code
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/linux-foundations/environment-variables-and-secrets

::o1 set a variable::Which line sets the shell variable REGION to eu-west in bash? {
=REGION\=eu-west #Correct. NAME\=value, with no spaces around \=, sets a shell variable.
~REGION \= eu-west #Not quite. With spaces, bash runs a command named REGION and passes it two words.
~$REGION\=eu-west #Not quite. $ reads a variable's value; you write the plain name when setting it.
~echo REGION\=eu-west #Not quite. echo only prints the text; nothing is set.
}

::o1 read a variable::REGION is set. Which command prints its value? {
=echo "$REGION" #Correct. $REGION expands to the value, and echo prints it.
~echo REGION #Not quite. Without $, echo prints the word REGION itself.
~export REGION #Not quite. export marks the variable for programs you start; it prints nothing.
~REGION #Not quite. bash tries to run a command named REGION.
}

::o2 unexported variable::You type DEBUG\=1 and then start ./server, which checks for DEBUG. The server reports that DEBUG is not set. Why? {
=DEBUG was set only in the shell and not exported, so ./server did not receive it #Correct. A program you start receives only exported variables; export DEBUG, or set it with export DEBUG\=1.
~./server can only read variables that are written in a .env file #Not quite. Programs read their environment; a .env file is a convention some programs load, not a requirement.
~The shell forgets variables after every command #Not quite. The shell keeps it; echo "$DEBUG" still prints 1.
~Variables must be in lowercase #Not quite. Case does not matter here; export does.
}

::o3 before the first commit::You are starting a project that will read an API key from a local .env file. When should .env be added to .gitignore? {
=Before .env is ever added to git, so git never tracks it #Correct. .gitignore only affects untracked files; once a file is tracked, ignoring it later does not stop git tracking it.
~Any time, because .gitignore also removes the file from earlier commits #Not quite. .gitignore never rewrites history.
~Only after the first push, so teammates get a copy first #Not quite. Pushing it would publish the key.
~Never; commit .env so teammates get the real value #Not quite. Commit a .env.example with placeholders instead, and share real values another way.
}

::o3 a committed secret::A real API key was pushed in a commit last week. Today you delete that line in a new commit. What else must you do? {
=Revoke or rotate the key, because it is still in the repository history #Correct. A secret that was ever committed counts as leaked; deleting the line does not remove it from earlier commits.
~Nothing; deleting the line removes it from the repository #Not quite. Earlier commits still hold the line, and anyone with the history can read it.
~Add the file to .gitignore #Not quite. .gitignore does not affect tracked files and does not change history.
~Rename the variable #Not quite. The old value stays in history whatever the variable is called.
}
